# The counter-proof register. One line per test: what made its checks go
# red, or that it is still owed.
#
# A check nobody has ever seen fail is not known to check anything. In
# one day seventeen were found here that had been green for months while
# testing nothing. So the thing a check is about gets broken on purpose,
# the test is run against that, and the red line is kept here word for
# word. Without the line an entry is a claim; with it, it is evidence.
#
# Two kinds of row, both tab-separated:
#
#   test    when    checks  what was broken     the red line
#   open    test    checks
#
# The second kind is the census of what is still owed, and it is the
# ratchet: a test that owes a counter-proof and stands in no row is new,
# and counterproof_test.py turns red naming it. The number may fall,
# never rise.
#
# The fingerprint under "checks" is what a row is tied to, not the file
# name: twelve characters over the wording of every judgement in that
# test. All of these tests are about to be renamed, and a row hanging on
# the name would be lost the moment its file was. So a renamed test
# keeps its row, and the name here is written straight the next time the
# suite runs.
#
# The two kinds are held to that differently, and on purpose. A proved
# entry whose judgements have been reworded no longer matches, and the
# test counts as unproved again -- a counter-proof from yesterday says
# nothing about a check rewritten today. An open test that is edited
# only has its fingerprint written straight and stays open: it owes a
# counter-proof either way, and nobody should have to produce one before
# they may touch it.
#
# A test that is renamed and rewritten in the same go matches neither
# way, and its row is reported as belonging to nothing. That is the one
# case where a hand has to put the register right, and it is a line.

